       These functions manipulate the soft function key labels that some hard‐
       ware  terminals support.  For those terminals that do not have soft la‐
       bels, _c_u_r_s_e_s takes over the bottom line of ssttddssccrr, reducing its  verti‐
       cal  size and the value of LLIINNEESS by one.  By default, _c_u_r_s_e_s uses eight
       labels of up to eight characters each.

       _n_c_u_r_s_e_s furthermore supports a mode comprising twelve labels of  up  to
       five  characters  each,  following a convention associated with the IBM
       PC/AT keyboard.  _n_c_u_r_s_e_s simulates this mode by taking over up  to  two
       lines  at the bottom of the screen; it does not try to use any hardware
       support for this mode.

   IInniittiiaalliizzaattiioonn
       ssllkk__iinniitt must be called before iinniittssccrr or nneewwtteerrmm.  If iinniittssccrr  eventu‐
       ally  uses  a line from ssttddssccrr to emulate the soft labels, then _f_m_t de‐
       termines how the labels are arranged on the screen.

       00  indicates a 3-2-3 arrangement of the labels.

       11  indicates a 4-4 arrangement

       22  indicates the PC-like 4-4-4 mode.

       33  is again the PC-like 4-4-4 mode, but in addition an  index  line  is
          generated,  helping  the  user to associate each label with its num‐
          bered function key.  LLIINNEESS and the vertical size of ssttddssccrr are  fur‐
          ther reduced.

